+   A maintenance pass resolves **every** committed object this way to build the set of fragments it must protect, and one object it cannot read leaves that set **incomplete** — which every pass reading it must then say out loud, because reclamation-safety and certification-honesty are the same property read twice. No pass that can **reclaim** bytes acts on an incomplete reference set: it deletes nothing, fleet-wide, since an unreadable map hides *which* chunks its object owns and no fragment can be shown not to be one of them. A pass that only **verifies** does not certify one either — it verifies every other object, names the one it could not, and reports the store **not certified** rather than clean. The operator-run pass that reconciles the fragment tier after a **metadata restore** answers the same way: it still reports every object it could read — what it marked collectable, and which chunks are unreadable, misplaced or under-replicated — names the ones it could not, and refuses to call the run clean, because a report drawn over part of a store is not a clean bill for it, and one damaged record must not leave an operator with no report at all at the moment they most need one. A **report-only** surface reading that set (a drain's reconciliation status) is held to the same rule and never answers "satisfied" over an incomplete one: telling an operator a server is safe to decommission is the reclamation decision in another form. That surface also **names the blocking record**, which is what distinguishes it from the ordinary "not yet" of a server still holding referenced fragments — one says an evacuation is running and will finish, the other that nothing will converge until a named record is repaired, and an operator who cannot tell them apart is left waiting on a stall with no way out. The containment is per object throughout — the damaged record is attributed and the walk continues, so one unreadable object costs the store one object's availability, not every healthy object's protection. Where a pass **can** read, it judges a chunk only on fragments attributable to the object that references it. Chunk-id uniqueness is a property of the allocator that minted the id, and the post-restore pass runs over a namespace just imported from a backup — minted under some other allocator state, reconciled against a fragment tier that was not restored with it — so it **observes** uniqueness rather than assuming it, which is free because the walk already reads one entry per committed chunk *reference*. Where it sees an id claimed by more than one committed reference, **no** byte bearing that id is evidence for any of them — not even one sitting at a claimant's own placement, since the other claimant may name that same server and is answered by the very same fragment. So each claimant's chunk is reported lost *for that object*, never merely misplaced (restaging those bytes would put one object's data under another's map), no copy of the id may be reclaimed, and a reclamation mark an earlier pass left on one is **withdrawn** rather than left for collection. The count is of **references**, not of the D servers they name, because two claimants that agree on a placement are exactly as ambiguous as two that disagree. A singly-claimed id — every id the shipped allocators mint, since a chunk id derives from an inode no live record shares or from a per-process random epoch — is judged exactly as before, including a fragment found away from its placement: the committed namespace cannot tell that from a stale fragment whose id a rewound allocator re-minted, so the pass reports it recoverable and says in the same breath that a restage must be confirmed against the object first. Narrowing the reuse is the allocator's own job, not the report's.
